{"id": "39359", "revid": "2218862", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39359", "title": "Williston (North Dakota)", "text": "Williston is a city on the Missouri River, near the northwestern corner of North Dakota, that is now experiencing explosive growth due to an oil boom. It is about 230 miles (370 km) northwest of Bismarck - the state capital - by car. Williston is the county seat. Its population grew from 15,000 in 2010 to about 27,000 in 2018.\nUnderstand.\nWilliston was founded as a Northern Pacific Railroad stop in 1887 and named for Daniel Willis James. It was chosen over the nearby town of Buford as the site of a turntable which led to its early growth. In 1951, oil was first discovered in the Williston Basin. Williston experienced rapid growth during its first oil boom in the 1980s. After the boom died down, the population shrank to about 13,000. Geologists have discovered 4.3 billion barrels of recoverable oil in the Bakken Formation. The Three Forks Salish Formation also contains recoverable oil. With these discoveries, Williston experienced another oil boom and rapidly increased in size. While the rest of the country remained in a recession, Williston boasted a strong economy, one of the lowest unemployment rates in the nation, plenty of jobs, and the highest wages in North Dakota. Following a sharp decline in oil prices in the second half of 2014, the boom ended (at least for the time being).\nDespite this growth, Williston still maintains a small town atmosphere. Most residents are friendly and will happily give visitors directions.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nUS Highway 2 runs east-west, intersecting with Interstate 29 in Grand Forks and Interstate 15 in Shelby, Montana.\nUS Highway 85 runs north-south, intersecting with Interstate 94 west of Dickinson.\nBuy.\nDowntown Williston offers many shopping opportunities.\nSleep.\nThe oil boom in Williston resulted in a severe housing shortage, but the situation has changed with the boom going bust. Many developments built to house workers during the boom now stand empty, and other developments were canceled. Booking a hotel room in Williston on short notice was difficult or impossible during the boom, with oil companies contracting with area hotels to house workers, but should now be considerably easier.\nMost motels are either clustered downtown or near the airport at the northern limit of town.\nStay safe.\nNorth Dakota has one of the lowest crime rates in North America, and Williston has one of the lowest of North Dakota. Most residents do not lock their doors, and in wintertime many people leave their cars running in parking lots as they run their errands.\nViolent crime and rape are rare, and usually occur due to the influence of alcohol. However, women should use common sense when walking alone near bars at night. Most residents and police officers will pull over and offer rides to people walking at night or in the winter. The biggest threat are drunk drivers. If needed, the police can be reached at \u260f."}
{"id": "39360", "revid": "2164467", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39360", "title": "Williston (Vermont)", "text": "Williston is a city in Northwest Vermont.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nWilliston is easily accessible by car using I-89 exit 12, US 2, and VT 2a. It's about 15 minutes from Burlington, 2 hours from Montreal, and 3.5 hours from Boston.\nBy train.\nThere is an Amtrak station in neighboring Essex Junction and a bus route that connects the two towns.\nBy plane.\nWilliston is about 10 minutes from Burlington International Airport.\nBuy.\nWilliston's retail options are mostly big box stores that you can find anywhere in the U.S., however there are a few local stores worth a look if you're in town. Canadians frequently visit Williston's shopping centers to take advantage of cheaper prices and lower taxes. If the large stores are what you're after, you will find Wal-Mart, Home Depot, Toys R Us, Dick's, Best Buy, Old Navy, Staples, Christmas Tree Shops, Bed Bath &amp; Beyond, Marshalls, AC Moore, and Petco.\nEat.\nYou can find national chains like Moes, Panera, 99, Chilis, Longhorn, and Texas Roadhouse in Williston, but then you'd be missing out on the local options."}
{"id": "39361", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39361", "title": "Willits", "text": "Willits is a city in Mendocino County on the North Coast of California. It is best known as the Heart of Mendocino County. It offers an amazing array of shopping and amusements for a city with a population of 5,000 in 2020.\nUnderstand.\nThe City of Willits began as a town of loggers and ranchers. In the wake of San Francisco's Summer of Love, an influx of hippies changed the character of the populace to today's mix: first, second, and third generation hippies coexisting with third, fourth, and fifth generation settlers. Add a sizable cohort of the artistically and liberally inclined with a certain tinge of New Age beliefs, and you definitely have a town with people watching possibilities while shopping the local galleries and craft shops.\nMany of these boutiques are not easily seen by the passing traveler. One has to stop and look about to wring the greatest satisfaction from the Willits experience.\nGet in.\nThe City of Willits is 135 miles north of San Francisco on Highway 101. The highway passes south to north, bisecting Willits as Main Street; highway addresses change to city addresses at the city line. In the center of town, just after passing under the Heart of the Redwoods arch over 101, Highway 20 runs its winding way westward to Fort Bragg.\nComing from the Sacramento Valley, take Williams exit 578 from I-5 westward onto California Highway 20. When it merges with Highway 101, turn north. Again, you are entering Willits from the south.\nGet around.\nA visitor to Willits will find the majority of its attractions are located the two blocks before the Commercial Street light, or just around the corner on Commercial Street as far as the Center for the Arts. Downtown Willits is easily walkable; this less than half mile flat loop will take you past an eclectic array of shops, eateries, and galleries.\nWillits' central location makes it an ideal hub from which to explore the rest of Mendocino County on day trips.\nSee.\nAttractions are listed from south to north.\nDo.\nBesides Willits' good old-fashioned walkable downtown featuring shops full of arts, crafts, and goodies, it also offers other attractions. Handily enough, the attractions begin in that same downtown block that ends at Commercial Street and Main Street.\nAn easy walk from shopping takes you to:\nThe other attractions are a bit further down; the traveler may want to drive to them:\nBuy.\nDowntown Willits offers travelers a beggar's dozen shops in two short blocks; a quarter mile stroll can reach them all. From south to north, and back again:"}
{"id": "39362", "revid": "2253620", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39362", "title": "Willmar", "text": "Willmar is a city in Minnesota."}
{"id": "39363", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39363", "title": "Willoughby", "text": "Willoughby is a town in Lake County in Ohio.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nI90 runs through Willoughby.\nGo next.\nExplore Cleveland or head up to Erie or Buffalo."}
{"id": "39364", "revid": "160739", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39364", "title": "Willow", "text": "There is more than one place called Willow:"}
{"id": "39365", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39365", "title": "Willow (Alaska)", "text": "Willow is an unincorporated community in Matanuska-Susitna Valley.\nGet in.\nWillow is easily accessible by car, being an approximately two-hour drive from Ted Stevens Anchorage International Airport ().\nGet around.\nIt is easiest to get around Willow by car or bicycle, but there are many lakes in the area and a crafty few travel by boat.\nBuy.\nWillow is not known as a tourist trap, but souvenirs can be bought at many shops in the area.\nSleep.\nThe nearest inn is a Best Western in Wasilla. A motel used to exist at the Capitol Speedway in north-central Willow, but it was shut down after a guest manufactured meth in one of its rooms."}
{"id": "39367", "revid": "160739", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39367", "title": "Willow Creek", "text": "Willow Creek is a small village in the Copper River Valley in Southcentral Alaska near the Wrangell-St. Elias National Park and Preserve.\nUnderstand.\nWillow Creek has a population of 201 residents.\nGet in.\nWillow Creek is on the Richardson Highway (State Highway 4) about midway between Copper Center and Tonsina. It is close to the intersection with the Old Edgerton Highway leading to Kenny Lake, which should not be confused with the Edgerton Highway (State Highway 10) that is the direct route to Kenny Lake, but intersects with the Richardson Highway further south, close to Tonsina."}
{"id": "39368", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39368", "title": "Willow Grove", "text": "Willow Grove is a town in the Philadelphia Region of Pennsylvania approximately 12 miles north of Philadelphia.\nThis article also covers the nearby communities of Horsham and Hatboro.\nGet in.\nThere are multiple ways to get into Willow Grove. There is a train station along SEPTA Regional Rail's Warminster Line in the heart of the town which runs to and from Center City Philadelphia as well as to several other suburbs around Philadelphia. There is also the Willow Grove interchange off of the Pennsylvania Turnpike toll road, which provides access to Willow Grove from the western suburbs of Philadelphia, Harrisburg, Pittsburgh, and other western points to the west and from lower Bucks County, New Jersey, and New York City from the east. Pennsylvania Route 611 is the main north-south road through Willow Grove, providing access from Philadelphia to the south and from Doylestown and central Bucks County to the north. SEPTA Bus lines also run in and out of Willow Grove from all over Philadelphia as well as the surrounding suburbs, with the Route 22 bus running daily between Olney Transportation Center in North Philadelphia and Warminster, the Route 55 bus running daily between Olney Transportation Center and Doylestown, the Route 95 bus running Monday-Saturday from Gulph Mills, and the Route 310 bus running Monday-Saturday from the business parks in Horsham.\nGet around.\nThe best way to get around Willow Grove would be from using your own personal vehicle, but if you do not have your own means of transportation, there are SEPTA bus lines that can take you around the town, as well as taxi services that you must call ahead of time if you would like them to pick you up.\nRide-hailing services Uber and Lyft can be used to get around Willow Grove.\nBuy.\nWillow Grove also has various shopping centers near the mall which include large stores such as Walmart and a Dick's Sporting Goods.\nGo next.\nWillow Grove has very easy access to local destinations and the Pennsylvania Turnpike. The hotels are right next to the Turnpike. From the main intersection of Willow Grove York Rd &amp; Easton Rd you can easily get to many different Philadelphia Region locations. Following Easton Rd (611) North heads towards the Turnpike or Doylestown. Following Easton Rd (611) South will get to Philadelphia."}
{"id": "39369", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39369", "title": "Willows", "text": "Willows is in Glenn County, California, in the Sacramento Valley.\nEat.\nAs is the case with most of Glenn County, there is not a lot to eat, or even see in Willows. If you are passing through and you want good food, there are a few options."}
{"id": "39370", "revid": "2253620", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39370", "title": "Wilmette", "text": "Wilmette is 16 miles (25 km) north of Chicago. It is part of the affluent corridor of lakefront Chicago suburbs known as the North Shore. It is the location of the world's oldest still-standing Bah\u00e1'\u00ed House of Worship.\nSleep.\nThere are no hotels in Wilmette. Try hotels in Evanston, Skokie, or Glenview."}
