{"id": "39556", "revid": "160739", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39556", "title": "Wood Dale", "text": "Wood Dale is a city in the Chicagoland region in Illinois."}
{"id": "39559", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39559", "title": "Woodinville", "text": "Woodinville is a city of 9,900 in King County of Washington State, 20 mi northeast of Seattle. It is the production center of Washington's wine industry, where many of the state's top wineries offer tasting rooms.\nUnderstand.\nWoodinville was founded in the late 19th century by Ira Woodin, and was a small logging town along the Sammamish River until the 1960s. The growth of Seattle caught up to it and it became a leafy and desirable suburb. In 1993, Woodinville was granted city status. Around that time, the longstanding Chateau Ste. Michelle Winery and newcomer Redhook Brewery became widely noticed, and additional wineries quickly sprouted up throughout the Sammamish River valley.\nGet in.\nBy car.\n State Route 522 (WA-522) runs through the northwest of the city. WA-522 connects to Interstate 405 (I-405) less than one mile to the west.\n State Route 202 (WA-202), signed as Redmond-Woodinville Road, comes in from Redmond to the south, through the Sammamish Valley.\nThe city is 20 minutes west of Duvall via Woodinville-Duvall Road.\nBy bus.\nNear downtown there is a park-and-ride with connections to metropolitan King County.\nBy bicycle.\nThe Burke-Gilman Trail runs along the western rim of Lake Washington through Seattle and Lake Forest Park, then along the Sammamish River through Kenmore, Bothell, Woodinville, and Redmond. Despite the hilly surroundings, the track is flat as it used to be a railway line.\nGet around.\nPublic transportation is few and far between so a car is recommended.\nSee.\nWineries and distilleries.\nAlthough the grapes are grown elsewhere, there are over 75 wineries in the city and surrounding areas. Most offer wine tastings and tours.\nWoodinville also has a growing distillery scene.\nBuy.\nWoodinville has several strip malls and is home to Target, Petsmart, Barnes &amp; Noble, Eddie Bauer, Office Max, TJ Maxx, Home Depot, Costco as well as several grocery stores and chain fast-food eateries.\nDrink.\nYou're never far from a wine tasting - the city features over 130 wineries.\nStay safe.\nCity Hall is downtown and contains the Woodinville Police Department."}
{"id": "39560", "revid": "160739", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39560", "title": "Woodland", "text": "There is more than one place called Woodland:"}
{"id": "39561", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39561", "title": "Woodland (California)", "text": "Woodland is a city of 61,032 (2020) in Yolo County in the Sacramento Valley of California.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nFrom the Bay Area take Highway 80 heading East toward Sacramento, then Highway 113 toward Davis. Exit Gibson Road or Main Street.\nFrom Sacramento take I-5 North toward Woodland/Redding and exit County Road 102 or Main Street.\nBy plane.\nSacramento International Airport () is 15 minutes away by car.\nBy bus.\nYolobus offers service from Davis, Winters, and Sacramento.\nGet around.\nYolobus travels inside Woodland, as well as to other cities."}
{"id": "39562", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39562", "title": "Woodland (Washington)", "text": "Woodland is a city in southwest Washington State, along the Lewis River.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe (FAA LID: W27) (for general aviation only) is SE of the junction of I-5 and SR-503.\nThe nearest airport for commercial passenger flights is Portland International Airport ()."}
{"id": "39566", "revid": "160739", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39566", "title": "Woodridge (Illinois)", "text": "Woodridge is a city in Illinois."}
{"id": "39567", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39567", "title": "Woods Hole", "text": "The village of Woods Hole administratively belongs to the town of Falmouth, in the Upper Cape area of Cape Cod, Massachusetts. Woods Hole has a great natural harbor and was once a fishing and whaling center but now is known for its beautiful beaches, sport fishing, quaint shops (no chain stores here) and marine research facilities.\nGet around.\nOn foot.\nWoods Hole is a wonderful place to walk around; everything is within 15 minutes' walk for people of normal fitness.\nBy bicycle.\nThe Shining Sea bikeway begins at the end of Railroad Ave, right next to the Steamship Terminal &amp; the Leeside Restaurant. It's a great way to get to Woods Hole from downtown Falmouth, about 4 miles, mostly along the ocean.\nBuy.\nThere are many small shops &amp; galleries in the village, many featuring local artists, all within a 5-minute walk. There is an ATM at the bank, at the top of Water St., right next to the U.S. Post Office and Pie in the Sky.\nSleep.\nMany private homes are rented by the week, month, and/or season. WHOI and MBL both maintain links on their websites listing local rental options\nGo next.\nMove on to other areas of Cape Cod or the Islands."}
{"id": "39568", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39568", "title": "Woodside", "text": "Woodside is a small town on the San Francisco Peninsula in California. It is considered one of the wealthiest towns in the United States, and is home to many celebrities and executives. Despite its small size, Woodside features many local attractions.\nGet in.\nSR 84, SR35 and I-280.\nGet around.\nPublic transportation is not available within Woodside due to its small size. However, SamTrans routes 78[dead link] and 278 make some stops not far from the town center, and route 85[dead link] stops at Alice's Restaurant.\nGo next.\nWoodside is fairly close to a few other communities on the Peninsula:"}
